# Machine output — never committed (unanchored: applies in every folder)
#
# NO TRAILING SLASH, for the reason spelled out at `glide/data` below: a
# trailing slash means "a DIRECTORY", and a SYMLINK to one is not a directory.
# `node_modules/` therefore ignored the folder and not a link named the same
# thing — so on 2026-09-10 `feat/ui-ux` merged a tracked symlink called
# `node_modules` pointing at `/Users/aurora/Developer/Claude/kwapso/
# kwapso_system/node_modules`, an absolute path on one person's laptop. On
# every other machine a fresh checkout of `main` got a dead link where the
# dependencies go, and every npm command, build and test resolved into
# nothing. It is the same trap the glide rule already carried a paragraph
# about, one directory up, which is why the paragraph is repeated here rather
# than cross-referenced: the person who adds the next ignore line reads the
# top of this file, not the middle.
node_modules
.next/
out/
# …AND THE SIBLING BUILD CACHES `NEXT_DIST_DIR` MAKES. `web/next.config.ts`
# documents that escape hatch as the way several agent sessions run `next dev`
# against one checkout at once ("set to a scratch path for a throwaway
# verification server"), and a scratch path beside the default one is named
# `.next-<something>` — which `.next/` above does not match. So a sibling
# session's verification build sat untracked in `web/`, one `git add -A` from
# a commit of several thousand generated files, and `oxlint` read every one of
# them: `npm run lint` runs FIRST in `npm run check`, so ANY session's check
# went red for as long as ANY other session had a verify server up. Ignored
# here and in `.oxlintrc.json`, because oxlint carries its own list.
.next-*/

# Local secrets + OS noise
.env*
# ...except the TEMPLATE. `.env*` correctly keeps every real value out of git, and
# for a while it kept the list of WHICH values exist out too — which is the half
# that cannot be reissued from a provider's dashboard. `.env.example` holds names
# and comments, never a value; the negation is anchored to that one filename so
# nothing else can slip back in behind it.
!.env.example
.DS_Store
*.log
*.tsbuildinfo

# Worker local secrets — keep out of git
.dev.vars
.wrangler/
.dev-state/

# The screen builder's built page — one regenerable HTML with React, the kit
# and the fonts inlined (megabytes). `node scripts/build-screen-builder.mjs`
# writes it; the catalogue beside it IS committed and locked by a test.
/tools/screen-builder/index.html

# Skill output — regenerable, never source. Each of these is written into the
# repo ROOT by a skill that re-runs on demand, which is exactly where canon
# lives, so an old run reads like a document someone wrote on purpose. The
# lean-mean and interface-lessness reports were deleted by hand once for that
# reason; ignoring them is what stops the next run putting them back.
/architecture-blueprint.html
/architecture-review.html
/architecture-review.md
/lean-mean-report.html
/lean-mean-report.md
/interface-lessness-report.html
/interface-lessness-report.md
/story-review.html
/story-review.md
/ocean-review.html
/ocean-review.md
/error-log-review.html
/error-log-review.md
/error-capture.json
/spend-review.html
/spend-review.md
# THE OTHER SEVEN. Twelve review skills say "write the report to the project
# root" in their own SKILL.md; this list had five of them, and the seven below
# were added on 2026-09-10 after `round_trip_review` reported a score and its
# file turned out to be excused by nothing. Run one of those seven and the very
# next `npm run check` goes red — on the DOC-MAP assertion, which tells you a
# document is not reachable from README, so the reader goes and edits README's
# doc map to link a file that should never have been read as a document at all.
#
# Hand-kept, and it cannot be otherwise: the skills live in ~/.claude/skills/,
# outside this repository (see /skills/ below), so nothing here can census them.
# To re-derive it when a review skill is added:
#   grep -l "project root as" ~/.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md
/activity-log-review.html
/activity-log-review.md
/base-fork-review.html
/base-fork-review.md
/dead-end-review.html
/dead-end-review.md
/first-run-review.html
/first-run-review.md
/realtime-review.html
/realtime-review.md
/round-trip-review.html
/round-trip-review.md
/speed-review.html
/speed-review.md
# `error_analyst` writes only the HTML to the root; its JSON is above.
/error-report.html
# NOT scaling-review.md/.html. README's doc map lists it as item 27 and
# DATA-MODEL.md cites it for a claim, so it is a DOCUMENT that two other
# documents depend on — ignoring it left the map pointing at a file a fresh
# clone does not have.

# WHERE scripts/backup.mjs PUTS THE BACKUPS, and the one line standing between a
# routine `git add -A` and every client's database in the repository. The script
# defaults to ./backups and the runbooks tell you to run it from the repo root,
# so without this the dumps land INSIDE the working tree — real customer rows,
# staged by the next commit that adds everything. The architecture review flagged
# it as the gap it would not close in a contended file; this is the file.
/backups/

# Per-workstream working notes — local to whoever is building, never forked
# into a new app (the new-app skill clones this repo).
# Session notes stay local (scratch, per-workstream memory) — but the LANES
# mailbox inside them is tracked: the briefs the planning and design lanes
# exchange need a commit, a diff and a history, which an ignored file cannot
# have (the design lane could read the first brief only because both worktrees
# share one filesystem). A dir-level ignore cannot be re-included, so the
# pattern ignores the CONTENTS and re-admits the one subfolder.
.session-notes/*
!.session-notes/lanes/
# A lane's SCREENSHOTS are evidence, not knowledge. They live inside the project
# because every folder must (the owner's rule), and out of git because the lane's
# own REPORT already says what each one shows — five megabytes of finished-lane
# PNGs would sit in the history for ever to save re-reading a paragraph.
.session-notes/lanes/shots/

# The pulled Glide rows are real customer data — the catalogue is tracked, the
# data never is. Re-pull with `node scripts/glide-pull.mjs`.
#
# NO TRAILING SLASH, on this and on the files rule below. A trailing slash means
# "a directory", and a SYMLINK to the data is not one — so `glide/data/` left a
# symlink named `glide/data` untracked-but-not-ignored, which is one `git add -A`
# away from committing the path to a client's whole export. A worktree is exactly
# where somebody reaches for that symlink, because the data lives in one checkout
# and the work happens in another.
glide/data
# The rescued Glide FILES — 79 MB of the clients' own logos, photos, PDFs and
# video, copied off Google's storage before the Glide account lapses. Same rule
# as the rows: the SCRIPTS that fetch and shape them are tracked, their output
# never is. `glide/files/manifest.json` maps every file back to the row that
# referenced it. BACK THIS UP SOMEWHERE REAL — once the Glide account is closed,
# re-running `node scripts/glide-files.mjs` gets nothing.
glide/files
# Derived from both, so it holds exactly the same customer data.
glide/normalised.json
# Where each rescued file now lives in OUR R2, and the row that referenced it —
# written by `node scripts/glide-to-r2.mjs <staging|production>`, and the map the
# migration will read to repoint those rows. Same customer data, one more shape.
glide/r2-manifest.json

# Parallel agent worktrees — working copies, never content
.claude/worktrees/
# The same thing under the owner's own rule of 8 Sep 2026: every folder this repo
# creates lives INSIDE the project, so lane worktrees moved here from
# ~/kwapso-lanes/. They are checkouts of branches that are already on GitHub —
# tracking them would commit the repo into itself.
.worktrees/

# The retrieval benchmark's corpus and its cached vectors — hundreds of MB,
# rebuildable by scripts/knowledge-retrieval-bench.mjs.
.kb-bench/

# Claude Code skills live globally in ~/.claude/skills/ — never vendored here
/skills/
.lane-shots-archive/

# real secrets uploaded locally for this session — never track
credentials/

# A lane running browser checks out of the primary checkout writes these into
# web/, which is one of the three directories this repo censuses hardest — the
# lint and the named-paths census both correctly refuse to walk past a file they
# cannot parse, and between them they blocked four merges on 11 Sep 2026.
# Scratch belongs in the lane worktree; this line is the seatbelt, not the rule.
.scratch-*
